1
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:04,540
You're here.

2
00:00:05,760 --> 00:00:07,380
Your organs are failing.

3
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:09,300
And your death is drawing near.

4
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:13,440
But if you bribe the right politicians in the right order at the right time,

5
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:16,600
you can trade the nuclear warheads for medicine.

6
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:20,080
And it's not even a crime.

7
00:01:40,284 --> 00:01:45,284
Wishocracy How to let billions of people say whether the next marginal public

8
00:01:45,284 --> 00:01:49,924
dollar should buy another nuclear weapon system or fund another pragmatic

9
00:01:49,924 --> 00:01:50,464
clinical trial,

10
00:01:51,524 --> 00:01:55,224
without routing the answer through lobbyists, committees, and middlemen.

11
00:01:55,804 --> 00:01:59,864
On Who Wants to Be a Millionaire, the studio audience picked the right answer

12
00:01:59,864 --> 00:02:01,944
ninety-one percent of the time.

13
00:02:02,384 --> 00:02:03,584
The "phone a friend" expert?

14
00:02:04,204 --> 00:02:05,044
Sixty-five percent.

15
00:02:05,844 --> 00:02:08,984
Your entire system of government is phone-a-friend.

16
00:02:09,524 --> 00:02:13,144
You phone five hundred and thirty-five members of Congress who don't know

17
00:02:13,144 --> 00:02:17,584
the subject, aren't trying to answer your question, and are being paid by

18
00:02:17,584 --> 00:02:19,084
one of the answer choices.

19
00:02:19,704 --> 00:02:22,124
Then you act surprised when the answer is wrong.

20
00:02:22,864 --> 00:02:27,124
On Wishonia, we found the same pattern four thousand, two hundred and ninety-seven

21
00:02:27,124 --> 00:02:28,524
years ago:

22
00:02:28,604 --> 00:02:33,104
random crowds outperform credentialed experts on every question where the

23
00:02:33,104 --> 00:02:35,164
expert has any incentive to lie,

24
00:02:35,784 --> 00:02:37,384
which in government is all of them.

25
00:02:38,104 --> 00:02:39,544
So we stopped phoning friends.

26
00:02:39,884 --> 00:02:41,634
We built a system that asks everyone.

27
00:02:42,584 --> 00:02:45,464
We named it after ourselves, which your species would call "branding.".

28
00:02:46,604 --> 00:02:51,684
Wishocracy replaces committees with code and representatives with mathematics.

29
00:02:52,104 --> 00:02:56,884
It lets eight billion people collectively decide how to allocate contested

30
00:02:56,884 --> 00:03:01,204
public budgets without having the answer quietly rewritten by lobbyists,

31
00:03:01,744 --> 00:03:05,264
committees, or people whose main qualification is seniority.

32
00:03:06,024 --> 00:03:10,344
The difference between this and Congress is like asking two hundred bureaucrats

33
00:03:10,344 --> 00:03:13,164
what you should have for lunch versus asking your stomach.

34
00:03:13,884 --> 00:03:17,804
Your stomach has never once funded a colleague's lunch out of professional

35
00:03:17,804 --> 00:03:18,504
courtesy.

36
00:03:19,484 --> 00:03:22,124
Your species is already making brutal tradeoffs.

37
00:03:22,844 --> 00:03:27,384
Every extra dollar for nuclear weapon systems is a dollar not spent on pragmatic

38
00:03:27,384 --> 00:03:32,364
clinical trials, pandemic defense, transit, housing, or anything else.

39
00:03:32,884 --> 00:03:37,364
Your current institutions hide that fact by slicing the budget into committees,

40
00:03:37,864 --> 00:03:41,644
subcommittees, and jargon until nobody can see the tradeoff clearly enough

41
00:03:41,644 --> 00:03:42,204
to object.

42
00:03:42,964 --> 00:03:44,984
Wishocracy drags it back into daylight.

43
00:03:46,084 --> 00:03:49,824
Ordinary polling is useless here because it lets people live in a fantasy

44
00:03:49,824 --> 00:03:51,784
where scarcity does not exist.

45
00:03:52,524 --> 00:03:56,964
Ask humans whether they want more spending on defense, healthcare, education,

46
00:03:57,044 --> 00:04:02,244
infrastructure, and also lower taxes, and many will cheerfully say yes to

47
00:04:02,244 --> 00:04:06,144
all of it because the poll never makes them pay for one preference with

48
00:04:06,144 --> 00:04:06,644
another.

49
00:04:07,224 --> 00:04:08,584
Wishocracy does.

50
00:04:09,404 --> 00:04:13,584
Every answer is a relative tradeoff under finite resources, which is why

51
00:04:13,584 --> 00:04:15,944
it measures budgets instead of vibes.

52
00:04:16,924 --> 00:04:19,804
How Wishocracy Allocates High-Level Priorities.

53
00:04:20,584 --> 00:04:23,024
Anyone can submit a priority proposal.

54
00:04:23,204 --> 00:04:27,524
Wishocracy allocates the part of a budget that genuinely requires collective

55
00:04:27,524 --> 00:04:28,024
judgment:

56
00:04:28,784 --> 00:04:32,824
cross-domain tradeoffs, shared infrastructure, and other public goods that

57
00:04:32,824 --> 00:04:34,464
markets handle badly.

58
00:04:34,764 --> 00:04:37,124
It's Kickstarter, but backed by the G.D.P.

59
00:04:37,324 --> 00:04:39,284
of nations instead of your friend's credit card.

60
00:04:40,584 --> 00:04:42,504
What Wishocracy Actually Decides:

61
00:04:43,224 --> 00:04:43,904
The hard stuff.

62
00:04:44,564 --> 00:04:48,864
Allocation across competing priorities where there is no honest price signal:

63
00:04:50,024 --> 00:04:51,664
High-Level Tradeoffs.

64
00:04:51,724 --> 00:04:55,884
"Nuclear weapon modernization" versus "Pragmatic clinical trials".

65
00:04:56,524 --> 00:04:59,004
"Pandemic surveillance" versus "Missile defense".

66
00:05:00,064 --> 00:05:04,564
"Warhead dismantlement verification" versus "Submarine recapitalization".

67
00:05:05,524 --> 00:05:07,904
Shared Public Goods Within a Domain.

68
00:05:07,984 --> 00:05:12,384
"Verification satellite network" vs "On-site inspection capacity".

69
00:05:13,244 --> 00:05:16,704
"Bridge inspection" vs "Port cybersecurity".

70
00:05:17,464 --> 00:05:18,744
"Trial matching infrastructure"

71
00:05:19,364 --> 00:05:21,924
vs "Research data interoperability".

72
00:05:23,164 --> 00:05:24,784
Everything else is execution.

73
00:05:25,284 --> 00:05:26,104
Engineers build.

74
00:05:26,664 --> 00:05:28,604
Operators operate.

75
00:05:28,604 --> 00:05:29,464
Researchers run studies.

76
00:05:30,304 --> 00:05:34,384
Markets and domain-specific institutions handle the parts where direct user

77
00:05:34,384 --> 00:05:36,704
choice or price signals actually work.

78
00:05:37,644 --> 00:05:41,664
Wishocracy governs the part where the species has to decide what the next

79
00:05:41,664 --> 00:05:43,484
marginal public dollar should buy.

80
00:05:44,804 --> 00:05:46,044
How It Works:

81
00:05:46,044 --> 00:05:47,644
Pairwise Slider Allocation.

82
00:05:48,484 --> 00:05:51,044
Your brain can't rank a list of twenty priorities.

83
00:05:51,624 --> 00:05:53,484
It gives up around item number seven.

84
00:05:53,964 --> 00:05:57,394
But it's much better at a local tradeoff between two things.

85
00:05:57,784 --> 00:06:02,224
So instead of asking "Rank these ten thousand priorities," the system shows

86
00:06:02,224 --> 00:06:03,884
you two at a time and asks:

87
00:06:04,544 --> 00:06:07,804
"How would you split one hundred dollars between these two priorities right

88
00:06:07,804 --> 00:06:08,304
now?"

89
00:06:08,924 --> 00:06:13,064
"Nuclear weapon modernization" vs "Pragmatic clinical trials"?

90
00:06:13,844 --> 00:06:16,504
"Pandemic surveillance" vs "Missile defense"?

91
00:06:17,344 --> 00:06:20,444
"Bridge inspection" vs "Port cybersecurity"?

92
00:06:21,084 --> 00:06:25,144
You drag the slider, submit your split, and do this a few times with different

93
00:06:25,144 --> 00:06:26,064
random pairs.

94
00:06:26,464 --> 00:06:29,904
It takes five minutes (less time than you spend choosing a Netflix show,

95
00:06:30,144 --> 00:06:31,744
and considerably more important).

96
00:06:32,704 --> 00:06:34,244
Millions of other people do the same.

97
00:06:34,884 --> 00:06:38,984
An algorithm aggregates all these pairwise allocations into funding weights.

98
00:06:39,604 --> 00:06:40,704
No filibustering.

99
00:06:40,784 --> 00:06:41,764
No horse-trading.

100
00:06:42,084 --> 00:06:46,184
No senators holding one priority hostage because they want a weapons plant,

101
00:06:46,704 --> 00:06:48,464
research park, or bridge in their district.

102
00:06:49,324 --> 00:06:51,224
Not everyone has to answer every pair.

103
00:06:51,704 --> 00:06:55,504
If you do not know enough about "pandemic surveillance vs missile defense,"

104
00:06:55,904 --> 00:06:56,464
you can skip it.

105
00:06:57,244 --> 00:06:59,644
This is how useful knowledge systems already work.

106
00:07:00,244 --> 00:07:04,944
A tiny minority writes Wikipedia articles, a somewhat larger minority edits

107
00:07:04,944 --> 00:07:08,104
them, and the overwhelming majority just reads.

108
00:07:08,684 --> 00:07:11,384
The crowd does not need everyone doing the same job.

109
00:07:11,724 --> 00:07:14,404
It needs enough informed people doing each job.

110
00:07:15,124 --> 00:07:17,744
Selective abstention is a feature, not a bug:

111
00:07:18,624 --> 00:07:22,264
better to skip a pair than inject random noise because you felt obliged

112
00:07:22,264 --> 00:07:23,244
to have an opinion.

113
00:07:24,184 --> 00:07:28,494
Low overall participation is still a risk, so the system needs thresholds,

114
00:07:28,904 --> 00:07:32,044
weighting, or fallback modes when engagement is too thin.

115
00:07:32,464 --> 00:07:36,724
But letting people answer where they actually know or care improves signal

116
00:07:36,724 --> 00:07:38,964
quality relative to mandatory guessing.

117
00:07:39,884 --> 00:07:43,024
One domain may still have its own internal allocation system.

118
00:07:43,764 --> 00:07:46,304
Health can have patient-directed trial participation.

119
00:07:47,184 --> 00:07:50,044
Science can have peer review or prize markets.

120
00:07:50,364 --> 00:07:51,844
Transit can have rider feedback.

121
00:07:52,724 --> 00:07:54,864
Markets can still handle things with prices.

122
00:07:55,464 --> 00:07:59,444
Wishocracy sits above that layer, steering the part that actually requires

123
00:07:59,444 --> 00:08:00,544
collective choice.

124
00:08:01,144 --> 00:08:03,584
One is the engine, the other is the steering wheel.

125
00:08:04,144 --> 00:08:06,864
You need both unless you enjoy driving into walls.

126
00:08:07,724 --> 00:08:10,424
Why This Actually Works (Math Warning).

127
00:08:11,044 --> 00:08:15,624
When millions of people make pairwise choices, something almost magical

128
00:08:15,644 --> 00:08:19,744
happens (by which I mean "statistically inevitable," which is the only kind

129
00:08:19,784 --> 00:08:20,604
of magic that works):

130
00:08:21,664 --> 00:08:24,484
Random pairs prevent gaming.

131
00:08:24,564 --> 00:08:28,484
Corrupting your current system requires buying access to five hundred and

132
00:08:28,484 --> 00:08:32,644
thirty-five members of Congress, which is so affordable that every major

133
00:08:32,644 --> 00:08:34,414
industry does it simultaneously.

134
00:08:35,444 --> 00:08:40,524
Corrupting Wishocracy would require bribing eight billion random voters,

135
00:08:41,164 --> 00:08:45,864
and the only way to "bribe" eight billion people is to offer them something

136
00:08:45,864 --> 00:08:49,284
they actually want, which is the system working correctly.

137
00:08:50,404 --> 00:08:54,884
The attack vector for Wishocracy is giving people what they voted for.

138
00:08:55,444 --> 00:08:58,364
Your security vulnerability is democracy.

139
00:08:59,504 --> 00:09:01,524
It's also immune to marketing:

140
00:09:01,584 --> 00:09:06,324
there's no ballot measure to link to, no "Vote Yes on Proposition Seven"

141
00:09:06,464 --> 00:09:06,964
ad to run.

142
00:09:07,604 --> 00:09:12,164
Each voter sees random pairs, so the only way to advertise is to convince

143
00:09:12,164 --> 00:09:16,124
people your proposal is better than whatever it happens to be compared against,

144
00:09:16,924 --> 00:09:17,424
which is just...

145
00:09:17,804 --> 00:09:19,104
making a good argument.

146
00:09:19,904 --> 00:09:22,964
Your species finds this deeply unsettling.

147
00:09:23,884 --> 00:09:27,784
Independence is structurally guaranteed.

148
00:09:27,844 --> 00:09:30,564
Surowiecki identified four conditions for crowd wisdom:

149
00:09:31,604 --> 00:09:33,124
diversity, independence,

150
00:09:33,804 --> 00:09:36,364
decentralization, and aggregation.

151
00:09:37,024 --> 00:09:40,364
Your elections destroy the most critical one.

152
00:09:41,184 --> 00:09:44,844
First-past-the-post voting forces strategic voting:

153
00:09:45,244 --> 00:09:50,024
people pick the "viable" candidate rather than the best one, so each ballot

154
00:09:50,024 --> 00:09:54,784
reflects what voters think other voters will do rather than what they actually

155
00:09:54,784 --> 00:09:55,304
want.

156
00:09:56,124 --> 00:09:57,724
It's an ant death spiral:

157
00:09:58,464 --> 00:10:02,304
each ant follows the one ahead, the leader follows the last, and the whole

158
00:10:02,304 --> 00:10:04,764
colony walks in a circle until it dies.

159
00:10:05,764 --> 00:10:10,104
Pairwise comparisons can't death-spiral because there's nothing to vote

160
00:10:10,184 --> 00:10:12,084
"strategically" about.

161
00:10:12,344 --> 00:10:13,124
"Which matters more:

162
00:10:13,903 --> 00:10:16,744
pragmatic trials or nuclear modernization?"

163
00:10:17,143 --> 00:10:22,064
has no spoiler candidate, no wasted vote, no reason to care what your neighbor

164
00:10:22,064 --> 00:10:22,564
picked.

165
00:10:22,903 --> 00:10:24,364
You just answer honestly.

166
00:10:25,264 --> 00:10:28,664
When millions of people answer honestly, you get wisdom.

167
00:10:29,244 --> 00:10:34,044
When millions of people game a two-option system, you get two candidates

168
00:10:34,044 --> 00:10:38,204
that three hundred and thirty million people chose from three hundred and

169
00:10:38,204 --> 00:10:41,604
thirty million people, and nobody likes either of them.

170
00:10:42,784 --> 00:10:47,784
Statistical models (Bradley-Terry, PageRank) extract global preferences

171
00:10:47,784 --> 00:10:49,404
from individual comparisons.

172
00:10:50,244 --> 00:10:54,744
The same math that ranks Google search results can rank humanity's budget

173
00:10:54,744 --> 00:10:55,464
priorities.

174
00:10:56,144 --> 00:10:58,514
Google uses it to find cat videos.

175
00:10:58,924 --> 00:11:03,044
You'll use it to decide whether the next marginal dollar buys a warhead

176
00:11:03,044 --> 00:11:04,284
or a clinical trial.

177
00:11:05,424 --> 00:11:05,924
Progress.

178
00:11:06,664 --> 00:11:07,944
Outliers cancel out.

179
00:11:08,504 --> 00:11:10,824
The guy who votes "fund my personal jetpack"

180
00:11:11,564 --> 00:11:15,184
gets drowned out by the millions voting for things that are at least attached

181
00:11:15,184 --> 00:11:16,224
to reality.

182
00:11:17,144 --> 00:11:19,154
Wisdom of crowds emerges.

183
00:11:19,764 --> 00:11:24,124
Remember the ninety-one percent versus sixty-five percent gap from *Millionaire*?

184
00:11:24,764 --> 00:11:30,264
Your species already proved that random people outperform credentialed individuals

185
00:11:30,264 --> 00:11:32,664
at picking the right answer.

186
00:11:32,804 --> 00:11:36,244
Wishocracy just applies this to questions that matter.

187
00:11:37,244 --> 00:11:38,444
Example with real numbers:

188
00:11:39,404 --> 00:11:40,564
five million people vote.

189
00:11:41,184 --> 00:11:42,624
Each makes twenty comparisons.

190
00:11:42,964 --> 00:11:44,924
That's one hundred million data points.

191
00:11:45,504 --> 00:11:47,664
Algorithm processes them in seconds.

192
00:11:48,184 --> 00:11:48,984
Output:

193
00:11:49,064 --> 00:11:52,884
"Allocate twenty-seven percent to nuclear weapon systems, forty-one percent

194
00:11:52,884 --> 00:11:57,564
to pragmatic clinical trials, eighteen percent to pandemic defense, fourteen

195
00:11:57,564 --> 00:11:59,144
percent to everything else...".

196
00:11:59,904 --> 00:12:02,764
No committee meetings were harmed in the making of this budget.

197
00:12:03,264 --> 00:12:04,864
It's democracy without the shouting.

198
00:12:05,364 --> 00:12:09,324
The stupidity is still there, but it's evenly distributed across millions

199
00:12:09,324 --> 00:12:11,464
of people and thus cancels out.

200
00:12:12,164 --> 00:12:14,164
Your species calls this "the wisdom of crowds."

201
00:12:14,824 --> 00:12:16,384
Mine calls it "obvious."

202
00:12:16,924 --> 00:12:20,444
About half of the eight hundred and forty-seven civilizations I've advised

203
00:12:20,444 --> 00:12:23,164
adopted some version of Wishocracy.

204
00:12:23,644 --> 00:12:27,184
The other half voted against it, which turned out to be the last thing they

205
00:12:27,184 --> 00:12:27,844
voted on.

206
00:12:28,544 --> 00:12:31,824
"But Direct Democracy Has Been Tried" (Yes.

207
00:12:32,564 --> 00:12:33,064
Badly.).

208
00:12:34,124 --> 00:12:35,624
You tried asking everyone.

209
00:12:35,884 --> 00:12:36,464
It went poorly.

210
00:12:37,084 --> 00:12:39,074
But not because asking was wrong.

211
00:12:39,304 --> 00:12:42,964
Your species has run four major experiments in direct democracy.

212
00:12:44,044 --> 00:12:47,224
Each one failed for a specific, diagnosable reason.

213
00:12:47,804 --> 00:12:49,944
None of those reasons apply here.

214
00:12:50,164 --> 00:12:55,464
Athens voted to execute Socrates and to invade Sicily (which destroyed their

215
00:12:55,464 --> 00:12:57,664
navy and lost the Peloponnesian War).

216
00:12:58,244 --> 00:13:00,304
The failure mode was mob rule:

217
00:13:00,384 --> 00:13:04,824
demagogues gave emotional speeches, crowds got excited, irreversible decisions

218
00:13:04,824 --> 00:13:06,954
happened before lunch.

219
00:13:07,224 --> 00:13:11,024
Wishocracy has no speeches, no candidates, no charisma.

220
00:13:11,544 --> 00:13:15,864
You cannot execute a philosopher with a pairwise comparison between infrastructure

221
00:13:15,864 --> 00:13:16,364
projects.

222
00:13:17,344 --> 00:13:22,264
California propositions let voters ban same-sex marriage (Prop eight, two

223
00:13:22,264 --> 00:13:26,204
thousand and eight) while spending over seven hundred million dollars per

224
00:13:26,224 --> 00:13:28,364
cycle on ballot measure campaigns.

225
00:13:28,644 --> 00:13:30,584
The failure mode was twofold:

226
00:13:30,584 --> 00:13:35,024
majority rule on fundamental rights, and unlimited advertising on binary

227
00:13:35,024 --> 00:13:36,504
yes/no questions.

228
00:13:37,204 --> 00:13:42,404
Wishocracy is scope-locked to bounded allocation questions (you cannot vote

229
00:13:42,404 --> 00:13:47,704
on who gets to marry) and presents random pairs (there is no ballot measure

230
00:13:47,704 --> 00:13:49,164
to buy ads for).

231
00:13:50,104 --> 00:13:54,564
Brexit reduced a one hundred-dimensional trade policy to a single binary

232
00:13:54,564 --> 00:13:55,164
question,

233
00:13:55,764 --> 00:14:00,084
then asked thirty-three million people to evaluate it with less information

234
00:14:00,084 --> 00:14:02,524
than they use to choose a phone plan.

235
00:14:03,124 --> 00:14:08,864
The failure mode was voter incompetence on complex policy forced into yes/no.

236
00:14:09,704 --> 00:14:15,394
Wishocracy asks "pragmatic clinical trials versus nuclear modernization,"

237
00:14:15,704 --> 00:14:18,524
not "leave a twenty-seven-country trade bloc:

238
00:14:19,084 --> 00:14:19,864
yes/no."

239
00:14:20,584 --> 00:14:25,044
Pairwise comparisons between concrete tradeoffs are the exact kind of question

240
00:14:25,044 --> 00:14:26,884
crowds answer well.

241
00:14:27,484 --> 00:14:31,684
Swiss referendums denied women the vote until nineteen seventy-one (one

242
00:14:31,684 --> 00:14:35,724
canton held out until a federal court forced the issue in nineteen ninety)

243
00:14:36,344 --> 00:14:39,904
and banned minaret construction in two thousand and nine with fifty-seven

244
00:14:39,904 --> 00:14:41,304
point five percent support.

245
00:14:42,164 --> 00:14:44,564
The failure mode was unbounded scope:

246
00:14:45,264 --> 00:14:48,784
any question could appear on the ballot, including questions about who counts

247
00:14:48,784 --> 00:14:49,284
as a person.

248
00:14:50,084 --> 00:14:53,644
But the Swiss system is also the closest working model for the parts that

249
00:14:53,744 --> 00:14:54,244
do work:

250
00:14:54,704 --> 00:14:56,704
turnout averages forty-five percent,

251
00:14:57,524 --> 00:15:00,274
decisions are granular, and the country has not collapsed.

252
00:15:01,084 --> 00:15:02,704
Wishocracy keeps the good parts

253
00:15:03,364 --> 00:15:07,344
(distributed decision-making, regular participation) and scope-locks away

254
00:15:07,344 --> 00:15:11,704
the bad parts (voting on rights, voter fatigue from fifty-page ballot packets).

255
00:15:12,964 --> 00:15:16,804
Five minutes of pairwise comparisons, not a weekend with a legal dictionary.

256
00:15:18,284 --> 00:15:23,044
Every criticism of direct democracy is actually a criticism of bad aggregation

257
00:15:23,044 --> 00:15:25,194
on unbounded questions.

258
00:15:25,444 --> 00:15:29,104
Athens failed because it used majority rule on everything with emotional

259
00:15:29,104 --> 00:15:29,784
manipulation.

260
00:15:30,704 --> 00:15:36,064
California failed because it uses binary yes slash no on complex policy

261
00:15:36,064 --> 00:15:37,744
with unlimited ad spending.

262
00:15:38,964 --> 00:15:44,444
Wishocracy uses pairwise comparisons on scope-locked questions with no advertising

263
00:15:44,444 --> 00:15:45,204
surface.

264
00:15:45,704 --> 00:15:48,244
Same species, different math, different result.

265
00:15:49,324 --> 00:15:53,244
Your Current System Is Phone-a-Friend (But Your Friend Is Being Bribed).

266
00:15:54,144 --> 00:15:58,244
That ninety-one percent-versus-sixty-five percent comparison is actually

267
00:15:58,244 --> 00:15:59,464
generous to your politicians.

268
00:16:00,484 --> 00:16:04,824
Phone-a-friend experts have three advantages over members of Congress:

269
00:16:04,864 --> 00:16:06,754
They're trying to get the right answer.

270
00:16:07,344 --> 00:16:11,524
A senator deciding between more nuclear modernization and more pragmatic

271
00:16:11,524 --> 00:16:14,284
clinical trials is trying to get re-elected.

272
00:16:14,744 --> 00:16:16,164
These are different objectives.

273
00:16:16,644 --> 00:16:20,004
One of them occasionally results in a sensible budget by accident.

274
00:16:20,344 --> 00:16:21,614
The other one does it on purpose.

275
00:16:22,464 --> 00:16:23,744
They're selected for knowledge.

276
00:16:24,124 --> 00:16:26,864
You phone your friend who knows history for history questions.

277
00:16:27,424 --> 00:16:31,124
Your appropriations chair got there through thirty years of seniority and

278
00:16:31,124 --> 00:16:32,304
prolific fundraising.

279
00:16:32,744 --> 00:16:36,884
At no point did anyone check whether they could price one extra warhead

280
00:16:36,884 --> 00:16:40,144
against one extra clinical trial. They're not being paid by one of the answer choices.

281
00:16:40,144 --> 00:16:42,104
If the phone-a-friend expert were receiving one hundred and twenty-seven million dollars from "Answer C," you'd call that corruption.

282
00:16:42,104 --> 00:16:44,044
When a senator receives money from a defense contractor and then votes on

283
00:16:44,044 --> 00:16:47,784
defense spending, you call that "lobbying," which is the same thing but

284
00:16:47,924 --> 00:16:51,524
with a syllable that makes it sound boring enough that nobody gets angry.

285
00:16:52,564 --> 00:16:55,324
So your current system isn't sixty-five percent.

286
00:16:55,704 --> 00:16:59,684
It's phone-a-friend where your friend doesn't know the subject, isn't trying

287
00:16:59,684 --> 00:17:03,044
to answer your question, and is being paid to say "B."

288
00:17:03,744 --> 00:17:08,444
Against that baseline, ninety-one percent seems less like a modest improvement

289
00:17:08,444 --> 00:17:11,624
and more like replacing a broken compass with G.P.S.

290
00:17:11,924 --> 00:17:15,974
The twenty-six-Point Gap Is a Floor, Not a Ceiling.

291
00:17:16,684 --> 00:17:19,264
Here's the part your economists should find uncomfortable.

292
00:17:20,064 --> 00:17:21,864
Trivia is the best case for experts.

293
00:17:22,584 --> 00:17:23,824
There's one correct answer.

294
00:17:24,164 --> 00:17:27,244
The expert either knows it or doesn't.

295
00:17:27,244 --> 00:17:31,064
Nobody is paying them to say "Constantinople" when the answer is "Istanbul."

296
00:17:31,204 --> 00:17:32,964
No lobbyist is taking them to dinner.

297
00:17:33,424 --> 00:17:35,644
Their career doesn't depend on getting it wrong.

298
00:17:36,024 --> 00:17:38,684
And they still only hit sixty-five percent.

299
00:17:39,464 --> 00:17:42,864
Governance decisions are worse for experts on every dimension:

300
00:17:43,484 --> 00:17:47,004
The "right answer" requires knowledge no individual has.

301
00:17:47,624 --> 00:17:51,464
Friedrich Hayek won a Nobel Prize in nineteen seventy-four for explaining

302
00:17:51,464 --> 00:17:51,964
why.

303
00:17:52,584 --> 00:17:56,484
No central planner, no matter how brilliant, can aggregate the dispersed

304
00:17:56,484 --> 00:18:00,644
knowledge that millions of people carry about their own priorities, their

305
00:18:00,644 --> 00:18:04,974
own risks, and their own willingness to trade one outcome for another.

306
00:18:05,144 --> 00:18:09,684
No committee knows how much the public actually values one less warhead

307
00:18:09,684 --> 00:18:12,604
relative to one more pragmatic clinical trial.

308
00:18:13,104 --> 00:18:17,024
But the public does, and a system that asks all of them knows more than

309
00:18:17,024 --> 00:18:18,484
any committee ever could.

310
00:18:19,184 --> 00:18:22,204
Experts have financial incentives to give the wrong answer.

311
00:18:22,724 --> 00:18:25,404
On *Millionaire*, nobody pays the expert to lie.

312
00:18:25,844 --> 00:18:27,784
In Congress, it's the business model.

313
00:18:28,244 --> 00:18:30,564
Experts are selected for the wrong criteria.

314
00:18:31,084 --> 00:18:33,264
On *Millionaire*, you pick your smartest friend.

315
00:18:33,744 --> 00:18:38,664
In Congress, the selection process optimizes for charisma, name recognition,

316
00:18:38,784 --> 00:18:43,104
and the ability to stand at a podium without saying anything career-ending.

317
00:18:43,844 --> 00:18:48,083
The overlap between this skill set and "knows which public goods deserve

318
00:18:48,083 --> 00:18:52,004
the next marginal dollar" is, as far as I can tell, zero.

319
00:18:52,624 --> 00:18:56,884
So if crowds outperform experts by twenty-six percentage points on questions

320
00:18:56,884 --> 00:19:01,664
where experts have every possible advantage, the gap on governance decisions,

321
00:19:01,904 --> 00:19:05,704
where experts are compromised, poorly selected, and missing most of the

322
00:19:05,704 --> 00:19:08,564
relevant information, is almost certainly larger.

323
00:19:09,164 --> 00:19:12,864
The ninety-one percent versus sixty-five percent gap is the floor.

324
00:19:13,264 --> 00:19:17,584
The ceiling is the Soviet Union, which was the most ambitious phone-a-friend

325
00:19:17,584 --> 00:19:19,274
experiment in history:

326
00:19:19,484 --> 00:19:23,744
a small group of credentialed experts making resource allocation decisions

327
00:19:23,744 --> 00:19:26,444
for two hundred and eighty-six million people.

328
00:19:27,044 --> 00:19:28,804
It ran for sixty-nine years.

329
00:19:29,324 --> 00:19:30,444
It did not go well.

330
00:19:31,244 --> 00:19:34,624
Why Markets Solved This (and Why You Still Need Wishocracy).

331
00:19:35,464 --> 00:19:37,944
Hayek's solution to the knowledge problem was markets.

332
00:19:38,584 --> 00:19:42,004
Prices aggregate dispersed information automatically:

333
00:19:42,004 --> 00:19:45,984
if millions of people want more of something, the price goes up, and producers

334
00:19:45,984 --> 00:19:47,064
make more.

335
00:19:47,204 --> 00:19:48,534
No committee required.

336
00:19:48,684 --> 00:19:52,344
Your species has been doing this for thousands of years and it works so

337
00:19:52,344 --> 00:19:56,404
well that you barely notice it, the way you barely notice breathing until

338
00:19:56,404 --> 00:19:58,204
someone puts a pillow over your face.

339
00:19:58,824 --> 00:20:00,324
But markets have a blind spot.

340
00:20:00,924 --> 00:20:03,704
They only work when someone can profit from the solution.

341
00:20:04,244 --> 00:20:05,974
Markets are good at shoes,

342
00:20:06,624 --> 00:20:11,064
mediocre at healthcare, and terrible at shared public goods like pandemic

343
00:20:11,064 --> 00:20:15,664
surveillance, arms reduction verification, basic research infrastructure,

344
00:20:16,104 --> 00:20:20,874
and anything where everyone benefits but nobody can capture the return privately.

345
00:20:21,244 --> 00:20:24,884
These are called public goods, and markets handle them the way your cat

346
00:20:24,884 --> 00:20:25,784
handles a bath.

347
00:20:26,524 --> 00:20:31,404
Wishocracy is what you build when you need Hayek-style information aggregation

348
00:20:31,404 --> 00:20:33,804
but can't rely on price signals.

349
00:20:34,484 --> 00:20:39,204
It takes the same principle (millions of individual decisions aggregated

350
00:20:39,204 --> 00:20:42,984
into collective intelligence) and applies it to the things markets won't

351
00:20:42,984 --> 00:20:43,524
touch.

352
00:20:44,124 --> 00:20:46,364
It's the free market's missing organ:

353
00:20:46,764 --> 00:20:50,404
the part that funds what's important instead of just what's profitable.

354
00:20:51,404 --> 00:20:54,244
Hayek diagnosed the disease in nineteen forty-five.

355
00:20:54,884 --> 00:20:58,204
On Wishonia, we built the cure several millennia earlier.

356
00:20:58,924 --> 00:20:59,604
Better late than never.

357
00:21:00,364 --> 00:21:04,044
From Priorities to Projects (Where Wishes Become Tasks).

358
00:21:05,064 --> 00:21:10,264
Great, so the Pairwise Slider Allocation tells you "Pragmatic clinical trials"

359
00:21:10,304 --> 00:21:12,644
matter more than "nuclear weapon modernization."

360
00:21:13,464 --> 00:21:13,964
Wonderful.

361
00:21:14,344 --> 00:21:14,844
Now what?

362
00:21:15,384 --> 00:21:18,304
Having a priority without a plan is just a wish.

363
00:21:18,684 --> 00:21:22,864
And wishes, as a rule, don't cure anything or dismantle anything.

364
00:21:23,544 --> 00:21:26,724
Wishocracy translates that priority into action.

365
00:21:27,304 --> 00:21:27,844
A.I.

366
00:21:27,864 --> 00:21:28,384
Breaks It Down:

367
00:21:28,924 --> 00:21:29,424
An A.I.

368
00:21:29,684 --> 00:21:34,144
takes the high-level priority and breaks it into thousands of smaller, concrete,

369
00:21:34,664 --> 00:21:36,224
fundable tasks.

370
00:21:36,264 --> 00:21:42,084
"Expand pragmatic clinical trials" becomes "Build trial matching infrastructure."

371
00:21:42,764 --> 00:21:45,724
That becomes "Integrate hospital data systems."

372
00:21:46,664 --> 00:21:51,784
"Build open verification tools for warhead dismantlement" becomes "Contract

373
00:21:51,784 --> 00:21:53,844
satellite imagery analysis."

374
00:21:54,764 --> 00:21:59,304
Every impossible problem is just a series of possible steps arranged in

375
00:21:59,304 --> 00:21:59,824
a line.

376
00:22:00,264 --> 00:22:04,424
Your species figured this out for building pyramids four thousand five hundred

377
00:22:04,424 --> 00:22:07,704
years ago but keeps forgetting to apply it to government.

378
00:22:08,604 --> 00:22:09,184
The Bounty Board:

379
00:22:10,004 --> 00:22:13,384
The system posts these tasks to a global marketplace.

380
00:22:13,744 --> 00:22:17,244
It's like eBay, but for whatever priority the public just chose.

381
00:22:17,944 --> 00:22:18,744
WANTED:

382
00:22:18,804 --> 00:22:20,964
Expand pragmatic clinical trial capacity.

383
00:22:21,464 --> 00:22:21,964
BOUNTY:

384
00:22:22,344 --> 00:22:23,264
ten billion dollars.

385
00:22:23,844 --> 00:22:24,344
WANTED:

386
00:22:24,764 --> 00:22:28,364
Build open verification tools for warhead dismantlement.

387
00:22:28,904 --> 00:22:29,404
BOUNTY:

388
00:22:29,704 --> 00:22:30,984
five hundred million dollars.

389
00:22:31,544 --> 00:22:32,704
The World Competes:

390
00:22:33,264 --> 00:22:36,284
The best teams from around the world, from M.I.T.

391
00:22:36,624 --> 00:22:40,594
to some kid in a garage in Lagos, bid on these tasks.

392
00:22:40,844 --> 00:22:43,604
The system funds multiple approaches in parallel.

393
00:22:43,944 --> 00:22:46,084
The ones that show promise get more funding.

394
00:22:46,404 --> 00:22:48,864
The ones that fail lose funding instantly.

395
00:22:49,624 --> 00:22:51,964
It's venture capital, but for public goods.

396
00:22:52,804 --> 00:22:56,584
Your species already uses this method to decide which restaurants survive

397
00:22:56,584 --> 00:22:58,624
and which phone apps get downloaded.

398
00:22:59,364 --> 00:23:03,144
Time to try it with the things you keep pretending are too important for

399
00:23:03,144 --> 00:23:03,764
competition.

400
00:23:04,484 --> 00:23:07,154
Why the Algorithm Is the Constitution.

401
00:23:08,124 --> 00:23:12,804
Your species has a habit of writing beautiful rules and then immediately

402
00:23:12,804 --> 00:23:14,364
finding people to break them.

403
00:23:14,904 --> 00:23:18,944
Your Constitution guarantees free speech, and your government classifies

404
00:23:18,944 --> 00:23:19,564
documents.

405
00:23:20,084 --> 00:23:24,484
Your laws prohibit bribery, and your lobbying industry does it with a receipt.

406
00:23:25,284 --> 00:23:29,804
Every protection you've ever designed relies on humans to enforce it, and

407
00:23:29,944 --> 00:23:34,184
humans can be bought, threatened, promoted, or distracted by a sufficiently

408
00:23:34,184 --> 00:23:35,284
interesting scandal.

409
00:23:36,204 --> 00:23:40,584
On Wishonia, we tried enforcement-based protections for about two hundred

410
00:23:40,584 --> 00:23:45,304
years before concluding that constitutional constraints enforced by people

411
00:23:45,304 --> 00:23:48,264
are just suggestions with better typography.

412
00:23:48,664 --> 00:23:50,644
So we put the protections in the math.

413
00:23:51,284 --> 00:23:55,564
Algorithmic Protections (Rules Nobody Can Break Because They're Not Rules).

414
00:23:56,304 --> 00:23:57,004
Scope.

415
00:23:57,024 --> 00:23:59,424
The domain is defined before the voting starts.

416
00:24:00,124 --> 00:24:04,344
An allocation ballot can ask "nuclear weapon systems or pragmatic clinical

417
00:24:04,344 --> 00:24:04,844
trials?"

418
00:24:05,464 --> 00:24:06,904
An arms-control ballot can ask

419
00:24:07,544 --> 00:24:10,584
"verification satellite network or on-site inspection capacity?"

420
00:24:11,604 --> 00:24:15,664
A health ballot can ask "trial matching infrastructure or research data

421
00:24:15,664 --> 00:24:16,724
interoperability?"

422
00:24:17,284 --> 00:24:21,004
It cannot ask who counts as a person, which religion gets banned,

423
00:24:21,704 --> 00:24:24,024
or whether your senator's cousin deserves a bridge contract.

424
00:24:25,224 --> 00:24:27,844
You can't deposit a sandwich at an A.T.M.

425
00:24:28,324 --> 00:24:30,724
The machine doesn't accept that input.

426
00:24:31,784 --> 00:24:32,864
Transparency.

427
00:24:32,904 --> 00:24:36,404
There is no backroom where a committee quietly edits the answer.

428
00:24:37,164 --> 00:24:40,744
All comparisons, allocations, and outputs can be published.

429
00:24:41,364 --> 00:24:45,164
Not because a law requires it, but because the system is designed without

430
00:24:45,164 --> 00:24:47,204
a "hide this part" button.

431
00:24:47,784 --> 00:24:48,344
Corruption resistance.

432
00:24:49,384 --> 00:24:53,364
Your current system can be captured by a manageable number of lobbyists

433
00:24:53,704 --> 00:24:57,884
bribing a manageable number of people in a manageable number of rooms.

434
00:24:58,524 --> 00:25:01,604
Wishocracy raises the cost dramatically.

435
00:25:01,764 --> 00:25:06,184
There is no single appropriations chair to buy, no single committee hearing

436
00:25:06,184 --> 00:25:09,644
to dominate, and no single swing vote to purchase.

437
00:25:10,084 --> 00:25:13,904
Most of the money never passes through a room with a door you can close.

438
00:25:14,904 --> 00:25:16,424
Bodily autonomy.

439
00:25:16,504 --> 00:25:18,144
You can vote to allocate budgets.

440
00:25:18,624 --> 00:25:21,984
You cannot vote to control someone else's body.

441
00:25:22,084 --> 00:25:23,354
Not by majority vote.

442
00:25:23,504 --> 00:25:25,024
Not by executive order.

443
00:25:25,024 --> 00:25:26,884
Not by a very convincing T.E.D.

444
00:25:27,044 --> 00:25:27,544
talk.

445
00:25:27,784 --> 00:25:31,524
On Wishonia, the shortest law on the books is still:

446
00:25:31,564 --> 00:25:32,064
"Self.".

447
00:25:33,384 --> 00:25:34,524
What Wishocracy Measures.

448
00:25:35,564 --> 00:25:37,144
Wishocracy measures one thing:

449
00:25:37,624 --> 00:25:39,884
how people want a bounded public budget split.

450
00:25:40,784 --> 00:25:43,644
It does not tell you whether the public is morally correct.

451
00:25:44,084 --> 00:25:46,924
It does not prove which policy is causally optimal.

452
00:25:47,824 --> 00:25:49,664
It does not replace evidence systems.

453
00:25:50,144 --> 00:25:54,064
It answers a narrower and more important question than your current institutions

454
00:25:54,064 --> 00:25:55,004
usually answer:

455
00:25:55,744 --> 00:25:59,744
what do people actually want the next marginal public dollar to buy?

456
00:26:00,364 --> 00:26:04,524
If the answer is "twenty-seven cents to nuclear weapon systems and seventy-three

457
00:26:04,524 --> 00:26:09,044
cents to pragmatic clinical trials," that is already more information than

458
00:26:09,144 --> 00:26:14,144
most elections produce after two years of campaigning, three billion dollars

459
00:26:14,144 --> 00:26:16,784
in ads, and a national shouting match.

460
00:26:29,244 --> 00:26:34,784
Thank you so much for taking the time to end war and disease, but if at

461
00:26:34,784 --> 00:26:39,004
any point you decide you're too busy, please press the share button on your

462
00:26:39,004 --> 00:26:42,704
glowing rectangle in case one of your friends have a free afternoon to end

463
00:26:42,704 --> 00:26:43,744
war and disease instead.

464
00:26:44,364 --> 00:26:48,704
And if you're too busy to even do that, please leave a really, really, really

465
00:26:48,704 --> 00:26:52,864
good rating and review so we overcome the pro -war algorithmic suppression

466
00:26:52,864 --> 00:26:56,424
and find someone with more spare time for ending wars and diseases.

467
00:26:56,984 --> 00:26:59,184
The good review doesn't have to be true, they don't check.

468
00:26:59,724 --> 00:27:03,784
But if you're too busy to even do that, the absolute least you could do

469
00:27:03,784 --> 00:27:09,404
is take 15 seconds to go to WarOnDisease.org and make your wishocratic allocation,

470
00:27:09,824 --> 00:27:13,384
telling us what fraction of humanity's resources should go to explosions

471
00:27:13,384 --> 00:27:14,844
versus clinical trials.

472
00:27:15,124 --> 00:27:15,624
And vote!

473
00:27:16,044 --> 00:27:20,304
If the 1% treaty passes, your vote will be personally to blame for saving

474
00:27:20,304 --> 00:27:24,924
2.6 lives and preventing 468,000 hours of suffering.

475
00:27:24,924 --> 00:27:27,624
Go to WarOnDisease.org right now.

476
00:27:28,024 --> 00:27:28,584
Love you, bye!
